Title: WELCOME 2 THE 'BURBS
Credit: Written by
Author: Martin A. Berryman
Draft date: 2018-06-11
Notes: Working draft. Fan sequel. Fountain format (https://fountain.io).

FADE IN:

EXT. MAYFIELD PLACE - DAY

A cul-de-sac in late autumn. Leaves, lawns, a faint sense of being watched. Thirty-five years older, but nobody told the street.

A rented DUMPSTER sits in the driveway of a tidy colonial. Its sides are already full of beige furniture.

RICKY BUTLER (56), rumpled, wearing a college sweatshirt that doesn't fit him the way it did, drags a mattress out the front door. It does not want to go.

RICKY
(to the mattress)
Thirty years, Mom. Thirty years on this thing.

He heaves. It lands in the dumpster, to a small round of nobody applauding.

He straightens, wipes his brow, and out of long habit looks across the street.

THE KLOPEK HOUSE.

Where once there was rot, peeling paint, and a lawn like a crime scene, now there is a perfect gray house with a perfect lawn. A small, tasteful sign by the door reads simply: THE OSTRANDER HOUSEHOLD.

RICKY (CONT'D)
No.

He pulls a PAIR OF BINOCULARS from the pocket of his sweatshirt. He has been carrying them for decades. We do not ask why.

INT. PETERSON HOUSE - KITCHEN - CONTINUOUS

DAVE PETERSON (50), neat, tired, standing at a counter with a laptop and a labeled bowl of oatmeal. Through the window, he sees Ricky on the lawn across the way, binoculars up.

DAVE
Absolutely not.

He closes the laptop and goes outside.

EXT. MAYFIELD PLACE - CONTINUOUS

Dave crosses the street and snatches the binoculars out of Ricky's hands.

DAVE
We are not doing this.

RICKY
Dave. Dave. Look at the house.

DAVE
It's a nice house.

RICKY
It's a nice house.

DAVE
That's what a nice house looks like.

RICKY
That's what a nice house looks like. Right there. That's the thing.

They both look at it for a long, uncomfortable beat.

DAVE
It was a dump last month.

RICKY
It was a dump last *week.*

A WINDOW SHADE in the Ostrander house lowers. Slowly. In unison. All of them.

Dave hands the binoculars back without a word.

EXT. RUMSFIELD HOUSE - FRONT YARD - LATER

An old flagpole. A very old man.

LT. MARK RUMSFIELD (90s) stands at attention beside it in full fatigues, saluting nothing. His caregiver CONNIE (late 20s), elegant, holding a thermos of tea, waits patiently.

CONNIE
Mark. It's lowered. We did it already.

RUMSFIELD
(not moving)
They're always watching, Connie.

CONNIE
It's a flag.

RUMSFIELD
I wasn't talking about the flag.

Rumsfield lowers his salute and turns his head toward the Ostrander house. Connie follows his gaze. Something flickers across her face that is not confusion.

CONNIE
(softly)
Well.

RUMSFIELD
You see it too.

CONNIE
(lightly)
Of course I see it. It's the only lawn on the street I'd consider.

She smiles. He doesn't. Neither of them is entirely joking.

FADE OUT.

TO BE CONTINUED...
